34. Dualism, Centre–Periphery Models and Cumulative Causation
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: IV | Topic: Dualism, centre-periphery models and the process of cumulative causation
Introduction
These theories argue that developing economies are structurally divided, and that market forces can widen rather than narrow inequality between regions and sectors.
Dualism
Dualism is the coexistence of two sectors with different technologies and behaviour within the same economy.
• Economic dualism (Boeke, 1953): a modern capitalist sector and a traditional subsistence sector, with different social systems.
• Lewis model (1954): unlimited supplies of labour in the traditional sector at a subsistence wage; the capitalist sector expands by reinvesting profits and absorbing labour until the surplus is exhausted (the Lewis turning point). Criticised for assuming no urban unemployment and zero marginal product in agriculture. Fei-Ranis extend it.
• Technological dualism: modern and traditional techniques in the same industry.
• Financial and geographic dualism: formal versus informal credit; urban versus rural.
• Features: wide productivity and income gaps, persistence over time, limited spillovers. Persistence suggests dual structures do not automatically merge.
Centre-periphery models
Prebisch and Singer: the world is divided into industrial centres and primary-exporting peripheries. Technical progress gains in the centre are retained as higher incomes, while in the periphery they are passed to consumers as lower prices, so the terms of trade deteriorate. The same logic applies within countries (core regions versus lagging regions).
Cumulative causation (Myrdal, 1957)
Gunnar Myrdal rejected the idea that markets tend toward equilibrium. A change triggers further changes in the same direction through a circular process, in a vicious or virtuous circle.
• Backwash effects: a rich region attracts capital, skilled labour and enterprise from poorer regions, widening the gap.
• Spread effects: growth in the centre creates demand and diffuses technology to the periphery. In poor countries backwash typically exceeds spread.
• Related: Hirschman's polarisation versus trickle-down effects; Perroux's growth poles; Kaldor's increasing returns and Verdoorn's law; Krugman's core-periphery in new economic geography.
• Examples: regional disparities in India (south and west versus east and north-east), and the rich-poor country gap.
Policy implications
Active state intervention is needed: regional development, infrastructure, strengthening spread effects, public investment, redistribution and integration of dual sectors.
Conclusion
Dualism and cumulative causation explain why inequality persists and why laissez-faire is insufficient, supporting a role for planned, balanced development.